-- title:
--   The two branches are the minimal primes of (UV)
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in W$, and assume that the quotient ring $W/(\pi)$ is an integral domain (a typeclass hypothesis `IsDomain (W ⧸ Ideal.span {π})`, which in particular forces it to be nontrivial). Work in the crossing model $R_\pi =$ `UVCrossingModel W π`, the quotient of the two-variable formal power series ring $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ (`MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) W`) by the ideal `uvCrossingIdeal W π` generated by the single element $X_0 X_1 - \pi$, and let `U π` and `V π` be the two distinguished elements of $R_\pi$ attached to $\pi$ by the project, which play the role of the coordinates of the two branches. The assertion is an equality of sets of ideals of $R_\pi$: the set of minimal primes over the principal ideal generated by the product $U_\pi V_\pi$, i.e. `(Ideal.span {U π * V π}).minimalPrimes`, consists of exactly the two principal ideals $(U_\pi)$ and $(V_\pi)$. Here membership in `minimalPrimes` unfolds to: the ideal is prime, it contains $(U_\pi V_\pi)$, and it is minimal among primes with that property.
--
--   This is the local two-branch statement for a crossing of two smooth divisors: over a base in which $\pi$ generates a prime ideal, the divisor $\{UV = 0\}$ in $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$ has exactly the two irreducible branches $(U)$ and $(V)$. It is used in the analysis of the completed local ring of the $\Gamma_0(p)$-level modular curve at a supersingular point, where it identifies the two components of the fibre over $\pi$, and is cited in the construction of the isomorphism of such a completion with the crossing model and of the corresponding description of its maximal ideal.
-- source:
